SOLUTIONS={
1:('FRAC-01','The rectangle is split into five equal parts. Three are shaded, so the shaded fraction is 3/5, or three-fifths.'),
2:('DIG-01','Write all the decimals in thousandths: 30, 48, 9, 5 and 20 thousandths. Five thousandths is smallest and hence closest to zero: 0.005.'),
3:('FRAC-02','There are 3 + 3 = 6 seats per row. To hold 150 passengers requires 150 ÷ 6 = 25 rows.'),
4:('RATE-01','Alice has 2 × 50 + 3 × 20 + 8 × 5 = 200 cents. David has 4 × 20 + 6 × 10 = 140 cents. Alice has 200 − 140 = 60 cents more.'),
5:('DIG-02','Arrange the digits in descending order to get 54321 and ascending order to get 12345. Subtract: 54321 − 12345 = 41976.'),
6:('FRAC-01','One-third of 75 g is 25 g. This is extra, so add it to the normal packet: 75 + 25 = 100 g.'),
7:('COUNT-06','Count by size, including triangles whose interiors contain other lines. Taking the smallest triangle side as one unit, there are 6 triangles of side 1, 1 of side 2, 3 of side 3, 3 of side 4 and 1 of side 5. Total: 6 + 1 + 3 + 3 + 1 = 14. Grouping by size prevents both omissions and double counting.'),
8:('ALG-04','Work backwards from 6. Undo subtracting 2 by adding 2: 8. Undo halving by doubling: 16. Undo adding 2 by subtracting 2: 14. Undo doubling by halving: 7. Check forwards: 7 → 14 → 16 → 8 → 6.'),
9:('FRAC-01','One-third is 0.333… . It is larger than 0.3 and smaller than 0.4, so it belongs between those two marks.'),
10:('GEO-02','Use the tiniest triangles as equal area units. The whole triangle contains 64. The three shaded triangles at the lower right each contain 4 units, giving 12. The lower-left group contributes 3 more units and the top contributes 1. Thus 16 of 64 units are shaded: 16/64 = 1/4.'),
11:('DIG-05','Work column by column in □2□ − 76 = 3□7. The units digit must be 3, because borrowing gives 13 − 6 = 7. The tens digit 2 has lent one, so borrow from the hundreds: 11 − 7 = 4. The hundreds digit must therefore be 4. The completed subtraction is 423 − 76 = 347, and the missing digits sum to 4 + 3 + 4 = 11.'),
12:('GEO-02','Draw the other diagonal. The two diagonals split the square into four right triangles with perpendicular legs 4 cm and 4 cm. Each has area ½ × 4 × 4 = 8 cm². Four of them give 32 cm².'),
13:('RATE-01','There are four journeys of 10 minutes, totalling 40 minutes. Before arriving at the final stop, the bus pauses at only the three intermediate stops, adding 9 minutes. Total elapsed time is 49 minutes; 10:35 am + 49 minutes = 11:24 am.'),
14:('FRAC-02','Eighteen cups contain 18 ÷ (2/3) = 27 recipe batches. Each batch makes 12 muffins, so the total is 27 × 12 = 324 muffins.'),
15:('GEO-02','A regular hexagon splits into six equal small equilateral triangles. Either original large triangle consists of those six plus three equal corner triangles: nine small triangles in all. The overlap therefore occupies 6/9 = 2/3 of one original triangle. Its area is 2/3 × 36 = 24 cm².'),
16:('DATA-01','There are 10 × 6 = 60 equally likely ordered outcomes. The pairs giving total 6 are (0,6), (1,5), (2,4), (3,3), (4,2) and (5,1). Six favourable outcomes out of 60 gives probability 1/10.'),
17:('ALG-03','Across all four possible toss results, each face number is counted twice. Thus the sum of the four face numbers is (10 + 11 + 12 + 13)/2 = 23. The two hidden faces sum to 23 − 5 − 6 = 12. They must be 4 and 8: placing 4 behind 5 and 8 behind 6 gives sums 11, 13, 10 and 12. Their product is 4 × 8 = 32.'),
18:('SOLID-01','Jessie’s square base is 80 by 80 cm and its height is 40 cm, giving volume 256000 cm³. Tom’s base is 40 by 40 cm with height 80 cm, giving 128000 cm³. Jessie’s patch needs twice as much soil, so choose D. Doubling two base dimensions but halving the height doubles the volume.'),
19:('DIV-01','The ages are consecutive integers. Since 31 × 32 = 992 but 32 × 33 = 1056, the first product over 1000 occurs next year at ages 32 and 33. Their present ages are 31 and 32, with product 992.'),
20:('ALG-01','Let the magazine price be p dollars. Jake has p − 2.80 and Joe has p − 2.60. Together they have p + 2.60, because that much remains after buying one magazine. Hence 2p − 5.40 = p + 2.60, giving p = $8. Check: $5.20 + $5.40 − $8 = $2.60.'),
21:('GEO-01','Let the shaded square’s side be s, and the left and right white widths be l and r. Then AB = s + r = 6 and CD = l + s = 8. Adding gives l + r + 2s = 14. This is exactly the rectangle’s width plus height. Its perimeter is twice that: 28 cm.'),
22:('FRAC-02','Let the total number of apples be A. Karen’s class has A/10 students. Both classes together have A/6 students. Jacqui’s class therefore has A/6 − A/10 = A/15 students. Sharing all A apples among A/15 students gives 15 apples each.'),
23:('GEO-01','The original eight-tile shape has perimeter 14. Adding a tile that shares one side increases the perimeter by 2; sharing two sides changes it by zero. For the shortest outline, fill the two end notches to form a 2-by-5 rectangle, still perimeter 14. For the longest, put the new tiles on separate exposed sides so each shares only one original side, giving 14 + 2 + 2 = 18. Their sum is 14 + 18 = 32.'),
24:('SOLID-03','Each vertex uses one face from each opposite pair. The smallest possible sum is 1 + 3 + 5 = 9, and the largest is 2 + 4 + 6 = 12. Such triples meet at vertices because none contains opposite faces. The difference is 12 − 9 = 3.'),
25:('DIG-02','Maximise the leftmost digit first while leaving enough later digits to fill six places. Keep the 9 from 9 and then the 9 from 19. You cannot next keep the 9 from 29, because only the two digits 30 follow it and three further places would be needed. The largest available third digit is the 8 from 28. Then keep the 9 from 29 and the final 30. The largest subsequence is 998930; its digit sum is 9 + 9 + 8 + 9 + 3 + 0 = 38.')}
